div.p:
Ex.
York, Archdeaconry
of.  Articles to be enquired of within the A. of Y. {29}

[25]  Some conventions are held by bodies which have no existence beyond the convention. If, however, they have a definite name, use that; ex., 4th National Quarantine 
                              {30}
                                and Sanitary Convention.  Often the name is given in different forms. Select that which appears to be the most authentic, and make references from the others.

Ex.
Ame  en peine, Une, not
Ame, Une, en peine. {73}

When the subarrangement of the catalogue is by dates (as in that of the Amer. Philos. Society), it may be well to place the date uniformly at the end of the line in this order: O. Wash., 1864. Otherwise the best order is to put the place and date immediately after the title, because like it they are taken from the title-page. The form, which is not copied but is the cataloguer’s own assertion, then comes last. The dates can be made prominent in a chronological arrangement by printing them in heavy type, as in Prof. Abbot’s “Literature of the doctrine of a future life.” In Very Short the German style of printing dates should be adopted, 742 (i.
                        e., 1742), 875 (i.
                        e., 1875). {76}

